      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 American Physical Therapy Association.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Current treatments for low back pain have small effects. A research priority is to identify patient characteristics associated with larger effects for specific interventions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to identify simple clinical characteristics of patients with chronic low back pain who would benefit more from either motor control exercises or graded activity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This study was a secondary analysis of the results of a randomized controlled trial."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "One hundred seventy-two patients with chronic low back pain were enrolled in the trial, which was conducted in Australian physical therapy clinics. The treatment consisted of 12 initial exercise sessions over an 8-week period and booster sessions at 4 and 10 months following randomization. The putative effect modifiers (psychosocial features, physical activity level, walking tolerance, and self-reported signs of clinical instability) were measured at baseline. Measures of pain and function (both measured on a 0-10 scale) were taken at baseline and at 2, 6, and 12 months by a blinded assessor."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Self-reported clinical instability was a statistically significant and clinically important modifier of treatment response for 12-month function (interaction: 2.72; 95% confidence interval=1.39 to 4.06). Participants with high scores on the clinical instability questionnaire (≥9) did 0.76 points better with motor control exercises, whereas those who had low scores (<9) did 1.93 points better with graded activity. Most other effect modifiers investigated did not appear to be useful in identifying preferential response to exercise type."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
            "content": "The psychometric properties of the instability questionnaire have not been fully tested."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "A simple 15-item questionnaire of features considered indicative of clinical instability can identify patients who respond best to either motor control exercises or graded activity."
          }
        ]
      },
